 electron-builder compiles the uninstaller and embeds it in the installer within one NSIS step, and SignPath signs only files a workflow step uploads. The `win.signtoolOptions.sign` hook is the one point between those two: in the first pass it copies the uninstaller out, in the second it replaces the freshly compiled uninstaller with the signed copy. Both come from the same NSIS script and version.
 
+The MittrCraft canonical builder also supports `node scripts/mittr/build-desktop.mjs --flavor dev --target zip` on macOS. This selects ZIP-only packaging while retaining every build stage and the final packed licence, brand, ripgrep, driver and VAD checks. Without `--target`, the default DMG and ZIP targets are unchanged. Both flavors exclude app-owned test/spec files and confirmed SDK/storage test fixtures from their staged server and dependency file sets. The staged server also leaves out type declarations (`*.d.ts`) and the modules only tests import: the Gitea, Gerrit and Forgejo fixtures, the fake and live Docker support and the place test suites under `lib/spaces`, and the folder-watch test helpers; a test fails when a packed module imports one of them. Third-party files and runtime assets merely named `fixtures` are not blanket-excluded. The staged server is the emitted runtime in `packages/web/build/server` (the workspace build writes it through `build:server`), packed in place of `node_modules/@openchamber/web/build/server`; required quota credential modules are checked in the build output, after staging, and in the final archive. The builder runs `prepare:opencode-server` and `verify:opencode-server`, and checks the packed `Resources/opencode-server` against the pinned `@opencode/server` version on Node without starting the app. It ships one ripgrep: the pinned release in `scripts/mittr/ripgrep.json`, staged as `Resources/ripgrep`. The OpenCode server's section of `brand/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` is written by `node scripts/mittr/engine-notices.mjs --write` from the packages whose code reaches the bundle and the native files beside it; the build fails when the section no longer matches them. Packages that are not under an open licence, and the rest of their npm scope, are left out of the section and printed by that command for a person to decide. Bun writes the absolute folder of each CommonJS module into the bundles, so after bundling the build replaces the repository folder with `/mittrcraft` (still an absolute path, which `createRequire` needs) and at the end fails when any packed text file names the repository or the home folder of the build machine. Upstream's `prepare:ripgrep` is not run, because it stages a different binary (`@vscode/ripgrep`'s own build of an older ripgrep, without its licence files). Its `afterPack` hook is `scripts/mittr/after-pack.cjs`: upstream's packed-contents check with the product file, splash logo and product plugins allowed at the app root, and the app-specific executable UUID, without upstream's icon catalog. Running a desktop test file directly, outside `bun run test`, needs `bun run build:server` first, because `@openchamber/web/server/*` resolves to that build unless the `openchamber-source` condition is set.
+
 ## Platform Notes
 
 macOS packaging needs Xcode/build tools for notarized builds and icon asset compilation.
